WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.960
NARRATOR: A controller is useful when a system
must reach and maintain a target despite

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.142
disturbances and uncertainty.

3
00:00:07.142 --> 00:00:11.977
NARRATOR: Imagine a motor commanded to run at
one thousand revolutions per minute.

4
00:00:11.977 --> 00:00:17.244
NARRATOR: An open-loop command may work under
one load and miss the target under another.

5
00:00:17.244 --> 00:00:19.897
NARRATOR: Feedback changes the structure of
the problem.

6
00:00:19.897 --> 00:00:24.846
NARRATOR: A sensor measures actual speed, the
controller compares it with the desired speed,

7
00:00:24.846 --> 00:00:27.321
and the difference becomes an error signal.

8
00:00:27.321 --> 00:00:30.803
NARRATOR: The controller uses that error to
change the motor input.

9
00:00:30.803 --> 00:00:36.260
NARRATOR: The new speed is measured again, so
correction is continuous rather than a

10
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:37.040
one-time guess.

11
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.882
NARRATOR: This lesson develops the
proportional, integral, and derivative terms,

12
00:00:41.882 --> 00:00:48.176
then connects their behavior to tuning, noise,
saturation, and a practical design workflow.

13
00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:50.725
NARRATOR: Start by naming every signal.

14
00:00:50.725 --> 00:00:53.621
NARRATOR: The reference r of t is the desired
behavior.

15
00:00:53.621 --> 00:00:57.230
NARRATOR: The measured output y of t is what
the sensor reports.

16
00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:02.332
NARRATOR: Their difference, e of t, equals
reference minus measured output.

17
00:01:02.332 --> 00:01:05.623
NARRATOR: The controller converts error into a
command u of t.

18
00:01:05.623 --> 00:01:11.086
NARRATOR: An actuator applies that command to
the plant, which is the physical or simulated

19
00:01:11.086 --> 00:01:12.179
system being controlled.

20
00:01:12.179 --> 00:01:17.426
NARRATOR: Disturbances can enter the plant,
while sensor noise can enter the measurement

21
00:01:17.426 --> 00:01:17.830
path.

22
00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:22.626
NARRATOR: This vocabulary prevents a common
mistake: the controller does not directly

23
00:01:22.626 --> 00:01:23.825
control the error.

24
00:01:23.825 --> 00:01:29.286
NARRATOR: It changes the plant input, observes
the resulting output, and thereby influences

25
00:01:29.286 --> 00:01:30.126
future error.

26
00:01:30.126 --> 00:01:32.613
NARRATOR: Sign conventions also matter.

27
00:01:32.613 --> 00:01:37.390
NARRATOR: With negative feedback, a positive
error should normally produce a correction

28
00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:40.177
that increases the output toward the
reference.

29
00:01:40.322 --> 00:01:44.034
NARRATOR: The proportional term responds to
the error that exists right now.

30
00:01:44.034 --> 00:01:47.886
NARRATOR: Its contribution is K p times e of
t.

31
00:01:47.886 --> 00:01:51.522
NARRATOR: If the error doubles, the
proportional correction doubles.

32
00:01:51.522 --> 00:01:57.705
NARRATOR: Increasing K p usually makes the
response more forceful and can reduce rise

33
00:01:57.705 --> 00:02:02.121
time, but excessive gain can produce
overshoot, oscillation, or instability.

34
00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:06.445
NARRATOR: Proportional action alone may also
leave a steady offset.

35
00:02:06.445 --> 00:02:12.926
NARRATOR: To hold a load against friction, for
example, the actuator may require a nonzero

36
00:02:12.926 --> 00:02:13.358
command.

37
00:02:13.358 --> 00:02:19.121
NARRATOR: If command is proportional to error,
a persistent error may be needed to produce

38
00:02:19.121 --> 00:02:19.889
that command.

39
00:02:19.889 --> 00:02:25.195
NARRATOR: This is not a defect in the formula;
it is a consequence of the loop and plant.

40
00:02:25.195 --> 00:02:30.004
NARRATOR: The important skill is to connect
gain to observed response rather than

41
00:02:30.004 --> 00:02:32.223
memorizing that larger is always better.

42
00:02:32.354 --> 00:02:35.505
NARRATOR: The integral term responds to the
history of error.

43
00:02:35.505 --> 00:02:40.569
NARRATOR: Its state grows while error has one
sign and shrinks when the sign reverses.

44
00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:45.960
NARRATOR: A small error that persists can
therefore produce a large enough correction to

45
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.656
eliminate the offset left by proportional
control.

46
00:02:48.656 --> 00:02:53.120
NARRATOR: This makes integral action powerful,
but it also introduces memory.

47
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.697
NARRATOR: If the actuator saturates at its
maximum while the error remains large, the

48
00:02:58.697 --> 00:03:03.477
integral state can continue accumulating even
though the plant cannot respond further.

49
00:03:03.477 --> 00:03:08.163
NARRATOR: When the system finally approaches
the target, that stored integral action may

50
00:03:08.163 --> 00:03:10.326
drive it far past the reference.

51
00:03:10.326 --> 00:03:12.393
NARRATOR: This is called integral windup.

52
00:03:12.393 --> 00:03:18.017
NARRATOR: Practical controllers limit, pause,
or back-calculate the integral state during

53
00:03:18.017 --> 00:03:18.528
saturation.

54
00:03:18.528 --> 00:03:24.277
NARRATOR: Integral action solves one problem
while creating a responsibility to manage its

55
00:03:24.277 --> 00:03:25.161
stored state.

56
00:03:25.298 --> 00:03:28.959
NARRATOR: The derivative term reacts to how
quickly the error is changing.

57
00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:34.640
NARRATOR: If the output is racing toward the
target, the error may still be positive but

58
00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:35.350
decreasing rapidly.

59
00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:40.831
NARRATOR: A derivative contribution can oppose
that motion before the target is crossed,

60
00:03:40.831 --> 00:03:42.939
adding damping and reducing overshoot.

61
00:03:42.939 --> 00:03:47.901
NARRATOR: The difficulty is that
differentiation amplifies rapid measurement

62
00:03:47.901 --> 00:03:50.107
changes, including sensor noise.

63
00:03:50.107 --> 00:03:56.076
NARRATOR: Real implementations therefore use a
filtered derivative rather than an ideal

64
00:03:56.076 --> 00:03:57.071
mathematical derivative.

65
00:03:57.071 --> 00:04:01.684
NARRATOR: Many controllers also differentiate
the measurement instead of the error, which

66
00:04:01.684 --> 00:04:05.528
avoids a large derivative kick when the
reference changes suddenly.

67
00:04:05.528 --> 00:04:08.232
NARRATOR: Derivative action is not mandatory.

68
00:04:08.232 --> 00:04:13.845
NARRATOR: A well-tuned PI controller is often
sufficient, especially when sensors are noisy

69
00:04:13.845 --> 00:04:16.867
or the plant already has adequate damping.

70
00:04:16.994 --> 00:04:21.944
NARRATOR: A PID controller adds the three
contributions into one command.

71
00:04:21.944 --> 00:04:26.678
NARRATOR: Proportional action handles present
error, integral action handles accumulated

72
00:04:26.678 --> 00:04:29.992
error, and derivative action responds to
trend.

73
00:04:29.992 --> 00:04:34.724
NARRATOR: These descriptions are useful, but
the terms interact through the same plant.

74
00:04:34.724 --> 00:04:39.924
NARRATOR: Increasing K p changes the response
that the integral and derivative terms

75
00:04:39.924 --> 00:04:40.324
observe.

76
00:04:40.324 --> 00:04:45.260
NARRATOR: Increasing K i may remove offset
while reducing stability margin.

77
00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:50.655
NARRATOR: Increasing K d may add damping while
increasing noise sensitivity.

78
00:04:50.655 --> 00:04:56.800
NARRATOR: The final command is also bounded by
actuator voltage, force, valve opening, or

79
00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:58.117
another physical limit.

80
00:04:58.117 --> 00:05:03.304
NARRATOR: A controller equation without
limits, sample time, sensor characteristics,

81
00:05:03.304 --> 00:05:05.898
and plant dynamics is incomplete.

82
00:05:05.898 --> 00:05:12.294
NARRATOR: Good tuning treats the loop as one
system rather than adjusting three independent

83
00:05:12.294 --> 00:05:13.665
knobs in isolation.

84
00:05:13.826 --> 00:05:19.770
NARRATOR: A step response turns vague words
like fast or stable into measurable criteria.

85
00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:24.018
NARRATOR: Rise time describes how quickly
output moves toward the new target.

86
00:05:24.018 --> 00:05:27.717
NARRATOR: Peak overshoot measures how far it
exceeds the target.

87
00:05:27.717 --> 00:05:32.819
NARRATOR: Settling time measures how long it
takes to remain inside an acceptable band.

88
00:05:32.819 --> 00:05:37.487
NARRATOR: Steady-state error is the remaining
difference after transients decay.

89
00:05:37.487 --> 00:05:39.426
NARRATOR: These measures can conflict.

90
00:05:39.426 --> 00:05:44.209
NARRATOR: A gain change that shortens rise
time may increase overshoot.

91
00:05:44.209 --> 00:05:48.839
NARRATOR: Integral action that removes final
error may lengthen settling.

92
00:05:48.839 --> 00:05:54.866
NARRATOR: Stability is the first requirement:
a fast response that grows without bound is

93
00:05:54.866 --> 00:05:55.727
not useful.

94
00:05:55.727 --> 00:06:00.367
NARRATOR: Before tuning, define which measures
matter for the application and what

95
00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:01.913
constraints cannot be violated.

96
00:06:01.913 --> 00:06:07.005
NARRATOR: A camera gimbal, temperature
chamber, and chemical flow controller should

97
00:06:07.005 --> 00:06:09.783
not share one generic tuning objective.

98
00:06:09.938 --> 00:06:14.594
NARRATOR: A disciplined tuning process begins
with safety and requirements.

99
00:06:14.594 --> 00:06:19.849
NARRATOR: Confirm sensor polarity, actuator
limits, and a safe test range.

100
00:06:19.849 --> 00:06:23.127
NARRATOR: Start with integral and derivative
action disabled.

101
00:06:23.127 --> 00:06:29.090
NARRATOR: Increase proportional gain until the
system responds clearly without unacceptable

102
00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:29.632
oscillation.

103
00:06:29.632 --> 00:06:34.643
NARRATOR: Add integral action gradually to
remove persistent error, while watching

104
00:06:34.643 --> 00:06:36.010
overshoot and windup.

105
00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:41.019
NARRATOR: Add derivative action only when
extra damping is needed and the measurement is

106
00:06:41.019 --> 00:06:42.808
clean enough to support it.

107
00:06:42.808 --> 00:06:48.908
NARRATOR: Then test reference changes,
disturbances, load variation, saturation, and

108
00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:49.518
noise.

109
00:06:49.518 --> 00:06:55.393
NARRATOR: This sequence is not a universal
proof of optimality, but it makes cause and

110
00:06:55.393 --> 00:06:56.176
effect observable.

111
00:06:56.176 --> 00:07:02.327
NARRATOR: More formal model-based methods can
calculate gains, yet every real implementation

112
00:07:02.327 --> 00:07:06.941
still requires validation against unmodeled
behavior and physical limits.

113
00:07:07.082 --> 00:07:12.831
NARRATOR: Most PID controllers run in
software, so the continuous equation becomes a

114
00:07:12.831 --> 00:07:13.715
sampled algorithm.

115
00:07:13.715 --> 00:07:19.286
NARRATOR: The sample period must be short
enough to observe relevant plant dynamics but

116
00:07:19.286 --> 00:07:21.674
not so short that noise dominates.

117
00:07:21.674 --> 00:07:24.417
NARRATOR: The integral becomes a running
numerical sum.

118
00:07:24.417 --> 00:07:28.996
NARRATOR: The derivative becomes a filtered
difference between samples.

119
00:07:28.996 --> 00:07:34.538
NARRATOR: Output clamping enforces actuator
limits, and anti-windup keeps the integral

120
00:07:34.538 --> 00:07:37.057
state consistent with those limits.

121
00:07:37.057 --> 00:07:41.526
NARRATOR: Systems that switch between manual
and automatic control also need bumpless

122
00:07:41.526 --> 00:07:46.368
transfer so the stored controller state does
not create a sudden command jump.

123
00:07:46.368 --> 00:07:53.617
NARRATOR: Record the sample time and exact
algorithm with the gains; K p, K i, and K d

124
00:07:53.617 --> 00:07:57.644
have meaning only within a defined
implementation and unit convention.

125
00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:01.686
NARRATOR: A reliable PID analysis answers six
questions.

126
00:08:01.686 --> 00:08:06.994
NARRATOR: What are the reference, measured
output, error, command, plant, and

127
00:08:06.994 --> 00:08:07.477
disturbances?

128
00:08:07.477 --> 00:08:12.744
NARRATOR: What behavior does proportional,
integral, and derivative action contribute in

129
00:08:12.744 --> 00:08:13.702
this loop?

130
00:08:13.702 --> 00:08:16.686
NARRATOR: Which performance measures define
success?

131
00:08:16.686 --> 00:08:19.378
NARRATOR: Where can the actuator or sensor
saturate?

132
00:08:19.378 --> 00:08:24.250
NARRATOR: How are sample time, filtering, and
anti-windup implemented?

133
00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:28.663
NARRATOR: And has the design been tested
across the real operating range?

134
00:08:28.663 --> 00:08:31.776
NARRATOR: The goal is not to use all three
letters.

135
00:08:31.776 --> 00:08:37.155
NARRATOR: The goal is a stable, understandable
controller that meets requirements with

136
00:08:37.155 --> 00:08:38.051
appropriate complexity.

137
00:08:38.051 --> 00:08:43.166
NARRATOR: Sometimes that is P, often PI, and
sometimes full PID.

138
00:08:43.166 --> 00:08:47.922
NARRATOR: If you can explain the loop and
predict the consequence of each change before

139
00:08:47.922 --> 00:08:52.043
turning a gain, you are tuning as an engineer
rather than searching blindly.
